But when she snapped at the
bee, then one stung her,

00:56:03.650 --> 00:56:05.187
I believe on her snout.

00:56:05.187 --> 00:56:07.400
But that is one thing when you're hiking

00:56:07.400 --> 00:56:10.364
in Southern California you
really have to be viligant.

00:56:10.364 --> 00:56:11.197
Viligant?

00:56:11.197 --> 00:56:12.964
Vigilant, vigilant about--
That's even better.

00:56:12.964 --> 00:56:15.180
But if you're in a
village you'd be viligant.

00:56:15.180 --> 00:56:16.013
Right.

00:56:17.320 --> 00:56:20.700
Yeah, I'm talking about
ignorance is bliss.

00:56:20.700 --> 00:56:22.710
I guess that's me on a
hike, but I was looking

00:56:22.710 --> 00:56:24.170
for rattlesnakes and that
took all of my energy.

00:56:24.170 --> 00:56:26.100
No but I am just saying--
But you're right.

00:56:26.100 --> 00:56:28.568
You gotta be prepared,
like do not aggravate

00:56:28.568 --> 00:56:31.180
or go close, once you see bees--

00:56:31.180 --> 00:56:32.013
Go away.

00:56:32.013 --> 00:56:34.560
Go away because they could be Africanized.

00:56:34.560 --> 00:56:36.070
Bonehead move on my part--
Then you're in trouble,

00:56:36.070 --> 00:56:37.780
they'll chase you for like a mile.

00:56:37.780 --> 00:56:40.480
Bees didn't chase us but
a few of 'em were swarming

00:56:40.480 --> 00:56:42.520
around Jade and she might
have got stung twice

00:56:42.520 --> 00:56:44.800
but definitely once on the snout,

00:56:44.800 --> 00:56:46.750
so we walk off the thing
and then we're gonna walk

00:56:46.750 --> 00:56:49.050
up the path a little bit
more 'cause I'm like,

00:56:51.300 --> 00:56:53.510
I wasn't thinking that
much of it, it's like oh,

00:56:53.510 --> 00:56:54.370
she got stung by a bee.

00:56:54.370 --> 00:56:57.240
If I got stung by a bee,
I would keep hiking.

00:56:57.240 --> 00:57:01.450
And then she lays down.

00:57:01.450 --> 00:57:05.070
I'm like this isn't good, she
never lays down on a hike.

00:57:05.070 --> 00:57:06.978
We put some water down,
she drank a lot of water

00:57:06.978 --> 00:57:08.900
and then come on let's go,

00:57:08.900 --> 00:57:12.060
and then she would not keep walking

00:57:12.060 --> 00:57:15.550
and I start to get concerned,
Christy's already concerned.

00:57:15.550 --> 00:57:19.417
She's got good intuition,
she's like "Yeah,

00:57:19.417 --> 00:57:21.237
"this isn't good, pick her up."

00:57:21.237 --> 00:57:23.680
And I pick her up, she's very floppy,

00:57:23.680 --> 00:57:27.380
just putting her head down,
I started to feel horrible

00:57:27.380 --> 00:57:29.453
that I led her to this bee patch.

00:57:31.631 --> 00:57:34.700
They're like strawberries
I guess and then I'm like--

00:57:34.700 --> 00:57:35.533
Found a bee patch.
Christy's like

00:57:35.533 --> 00:57:36.707
let's turn around.

00:57:36.707 --> 00:57:40.700
We gotta get back to the car,
she might need treatment,

00:57:40.700 --> 00:57:42.850
and we walk a little
bit and I put her down

00:57:42.850 --> 00:57:45.520
and this has been like five
minutes since she was stung,

00:57:45.520 --> 00:57:48.443
we had decided to turn
around and she threw up.

00:57:49.540 --> 00:57:53.130
And then I'm like she could be
having an allergic reaction.

00:57:53.130 --> 00:57:54.910
Is there any swelling?

00:57:54.910 --> 00:57:57.460
There was no visual of
swelling on the snout

00:57:57.460 --> 00:58:02.460
or anywhere, just lethargy,
vomiting, and floppiness.

00:58:04.720 --> 00:58:08.050
And I'm starting to get scared
but I'm kinda in denial,

00:58:08.050 --> 00:58:10.157
I'm like I don't wanna
think the worst here

00:58:10.157 --> 00:58:13.387
but Christy's like her
throat can swell up,

00:58:13.387 --> 00:58:14.840
she could die.

00:58:14.840 --> 00:58:17.730
If she's having an allergic
reaction so we're walking down,

00:58:17.730 --> 00:58:22.470
we're calling the vet and
Googling as we're walking,

00:58:22.470 --> 00:58:27.470
I'm carrying her, she's like
a lifeless little fur ball

00:58:29.400 --> 00:58:31.607
and we're getting scared and they said,

00:58:31.607 --> 00:58:34.447
"Well the vet's in surgery,"
and Christy's like,

00:58:34.447 --> 00:58:37.360
"Well, can you recommend
somewhere else that we can go?"

00:58:37.360 --> 00:58:39.287
There was another vet that
we used to go to in town

00:58:39.287 --> 00:58:40.940
and she's like we'll
call 'em and it was like,

00:58:40.940 --> 00:58:43.100
no they're in surgery as well.

00:58:43.100 --> 00:58:46.970
I'm like dang, vets like to
do this afternoon surgery.

00:58:46.970 --> 00:58:48.530
That's where the big money's at.

00:58:48.530 --> 00:58:50.450
The vet agreed to come out
of surgery and take a look

00:58:50.450 --> 00:58:55.450
so we take her there and
the stinger for like,

00:58:56.570 --> 00:58:58.880
if it stays in there can
continue to pump venom

00:58:58.880 --> 00:59:00.430
even after the bee's gone away.

00:59:01.430 --> 00:59:04.070
I learned that you should
try to get rid of the stinger

00:59:04.070 --> 00:59:08.780
but it wasn't there, I think
she batted it away initially

00:59:08.780 --> 00:59:11.060
but she was having an
allergic reaction because

00:59:11.960 --> 00:59:13.650
the way she was acting and vomiting

00:59:13.650 --> 00:59:18.300
and they gave her
Benadryl and just told her

00:59:18.300 --> 00:59:22.370
she's gonna be really
sleepy and it was like,

00:59:22.370 --> 00:59:25.337
there was no sign of
swelling so they were like,

00:59:25.337 --> 00:59:26.640
"Are you sure she was stung by a bee?"

00:59:26.640 --> 00:59:28.453
We're like yes, definitely but,

00:59:30.358 --> 00:59:35.358
I didn't allow myself to believe
that this could be a life

00:59:35.790 --> 00:59:39.310
or death situation but I was
certainly hiking very fast

00:59:39.310 --> 00:59:42.020
back down to the car
to get her over there.

00:59:42.020 --> 00:59:45.640
So it was pretty scary
but they put us at ease

00:59:45.640 --> 00:59:47.210
and how to talk to the vet

00:59:47.210 --> 00:59:50.960
and then she was just very lethargic

00:59:50.960 --> 00:59:53.180
for the next 24 hours
because of the Benadryl

00:59:53.180 --> 00:59:55.810
but everything's fine now,
she's eating and drinking fine

00:59:55.810 --> 00:59:57.640
and look, she's right here.

00:59:57.640 --> 01:00:00.063
But she has a specific allergy to bees?

01:00:01.120 --> 01:00:03.553
Or this would happen
to any dog of her size?

01:00:03.553 --> 01:00:06.873
No they said it was an allergy
for her to react that way.

01:00:08.610 --> 01:00:10.513
I love you too, she gave me a kiss.

01:00:11.850 --> 01:00:15.020
She's still a little,
it's been like 48 hours,

01:00:15.020 --> 01:00:19.803
she's still a little bit mopey
but she's kinda mopey anyway.

01:00:20.870 --> 01:00:22.460
Look at her.

01:00:22.460 --> 01:00:24.260
She seems embarrassed.

01:00:24.260 --> 01:00:26.840
It wasn't your fault,
baby, it was my fault.

01:00:26.840 --> 01:00:29.696
I shouldn't have let you
into that patch of bees.

01:00:29.696 --> 01:00:31.890
I should have known better
and then but you talk

01:00:31.890 --> 01:00:34.560
about hot tip, he said,
"Where did you go?"

01:00:34.560 --> 01:00:37.077
and we told the vet and he said,
"Well you need to come back

01:00:37.077 --> 01:00:42.077
"and get a rattlesnake venom
vaccine," which I was like,

01:00:43.907 --> 01:00:45.977
"I didn't know this
existed," and they were like,

01:00:45.977 --> 01:00:47.417
"Yes, rattlesnakes are really bad.

01:00:47.417 --> 01:00:50.467
"There's a lot more rain in
LA now, they're coming out.

01:00:50.467 --> 01:00:53.097
"Dogs are coming in from
dog parks getting bit

01:00:53.097 --> 01:00:56.890
"by rattlesnakes in dog parks and dying."

01:00:56.890 --> 01:00:58.250
Hold on, you can get a rattlesnake--

01:00:58.250 --> 01:01:00.170
A rattlesnake vaccine.
Vaccine?

01:01:00.170 --> 01:01:01.003
Can I get one?

01:01:01.003 --> 01:01:03.300
He said that, well, he's a vet,

01:01:03.300 --> 01:01:04.500
he doesn't give it to humans.

01:01:04.500 --> 01:01:07.313
I didn't ask that question
so I don't know the answer

01:01:07.313 --> 01:01:12.313
but he said that all it
does is gain you time,

01:01:12.870 --> 01:01:15.980
like an hour to get them to a vet.

01:01:15.980 --> 01:01:18.020
That's what the vaccine does.

01:01:18.020 --> 01:01:21.320
So if you're in an area with rattlesnakes,

01:01:21.320 --> 01:01:24.050
there is such a thing
as a rattlesnake vaccine

01:01:24.050 --> 01:01:27.760
and it gains them precious
time to get them to a vet,

01:01:27.760 --> 01:01:29.570
I'm talkin' an hour.

01:01:29.570 --> 01:01:31.060
'Cause their bodies can't take it.

01:01:31.060 --> 01:01:33.000
If a small dog gets bit by a rattlesnake--

01:01:33.000 --> 01:01:34.500
I'm just not gonna take her out anymore.

01:01:34.500 --> 01:01:37.150
I think it kinda--
Keep her in a bubble man.

01:01:37.150 --> 01:01:38.950
Keep her in a bubble.
It's a little different

01:01:38.950 --> 01:01:41.870
than a child, it's like, our
children you gotta let 'em out

01:01:41.870 --> 01:01:44.057
into the world and educate them about--

01:01:44.057 --> 01:01:44.930
'Cause they gotta take
care of themselves someday.

01:01:44.930 --> 01:01:47.187
The rattlesnakes and make their
own place smell like feet.

01:01:47.187 --> 01:01:49.200
You're gonna take care of her.

01:01:49.200 --> 01:01:50.400
You can baby her all you want.

01:01:50.400 --> 01:01:52.440
That's right, that's why I walk her up

01:01:52.440 --> 01:01:55.330
and down the stairs, put her in the bed,

01:01:55.330 --> 01:01:57.020
I take her out of the bed.

01:01:57.020 --> 01:01:59.260
She'll jump out of the
bed now and she'll beg

01:01:59.260 --> 01:02:00.630
for water in the middle of the night.

01:02:00.630 --> 01:02:02.050
Right.
And so I start puttin' water

01:02:02.050 --> 01:02:04.150
in our bathroom upstairs 'cause I live

01:02:04.150 --> 01:02:05.733
in a huge frickin' mansion.
Yeah takes forever to get--

01:02:05.733 --> 01:02:07.510
It's a long ways down there to shoes.

01:02:07.510 --> 01:02:08.610
Right, getting to your closet.

01:02:08.610 --> 01:02:10.105
And water.

01:02:10.105 --> 01:02:11.440
Was that the same podcast?

01:02:11.440 --> 01:02:12.273
Are we still in the same podcast?

01:02:12.273 --> 01:02:13.671
Yeah yeah yeah, I mean one might argue

01:02:13.671 --> 01:02:17.150
that I should encourage
Barbara to go out on her own

01:02:17.150 --> 01:02:19.450
and go and be with another
family because she's gonna have

01:02:19.450 --> 01:02:21.150
to do that one day but she's
not gonna do that one day.

01:02:21.150 --> 01:02:24.142
She is not, but she is gonna die.

01:02:24.142 --> 01:02:26.330
I would have been--
After she lives

01:02:26.330 --> 01:02:27.260
her very long life.

01:02:27.260 --> 01:02:30.690
I thought about, I love Jade so much,

01:02:30.690 --> 01:02:31.690
I think I've said this.

01:02:31.690 --> 01:02:36.090
I frequently in my most
tender moments of snuggling,

01:02:36.090 --> 01:02:38.090
I just instinctively think
about when she's gone

01:02:38.090 --> 01:02:42.390
because it's like 15 years,
maybe less, if I'm lucky,

01:02:42.390 --> 01:02:44.120
she's gonna pass on.

01:02:44.120 --> 01:02:48.770
She's gonna go to doggy heaven, or hell.

01:02:48.770 --> 01:02:50.220
She may go to doggy hell.

01:02:50.220 --> 01:02:53.640
I don't know what the criteria
is for these decisions.

01:02:53.640 --> 01:02:54.960
Yours is definitely going to hell.

01:02:54.960 --> 01:02:57.470
But she's so lazy.

01:02:57.470 --> 01:02:59.610
She's mean though, she's mean.

01:02:59.610 --> 01:03:01.040
She's mean to other--
Not to me.

01:03:01.040 --> 01:03:02.190
Barbara's very nice to other people.

01:03:02.190 --> 01:03:04.690
She's going to doggy heaven.

01:03:04.690 --> 01:03:06.407
It's not like the woman who picked her up

01:03:06.407 --> 01:03:10.200
wasn't a literal angel or God.

01:03:10.200 --> 01:03:11.914
It was just a neighbor of yours.

01:03:11.914 --> 01:03:14.430
That woman has no bearing
on whether your dog goes

01:03:14.430 --> 01:03:16.160
to heaven or hell but I
find myself thinking--

01:03:16.160 --> 01:03:18.940
You haven't lost a dog
as an adult, I have.

01:03:18.940 --> 01:03:21.351
'Cause you haven't had a dog in so--

01:03:21.351 --> 01:03:23.551
If Jade would have died from the honeybee,

01:03:25.070 --> 01:03:27.240
I literally thought when
we got her back home,

01:03:27.240 --> 01:03:29.190
I was like if that would have happened,

01:03:30.040 --> 01:03:33.166
I would have been out of
commission for at least

01:03:33.166 --> 01:03:38.130
a solid week of I'm not
leaving the house, I'm not--

01:03:38.130 --> 01:03:39.600
I don't believe that.

01:03:39.600 --> 01:03:42.460
I think you would be in
mourning for a long time

01:03:42.460 --> 01:03:44.200
but I don't think you
would be like I can't come

01:03:44.200 --> 01:03:45.480
into work for a week.

01:03:45.480 --> 01:03:48.100
I think you probably would--
That's what I thought.

01:03:48.100 --> 01:03:49.445
That's what I feel but--
I'm not saying

01:03:49.445 --> 01:03:51.050
it would be easy.
I do think you're right

01:03:51.050 --> 01:03:52.870
that I would say I gotta get out of here,

01:03:52.870 --> 01:03:53.900
I gotta try to--

01:03:53.900 --> 01:03:55.710
Now in the Book of Mythicality,

01:03:55.710 --> 01:03:57.000
I talk extensively about this,

01:03:57.000 --> 01:03:59.380
about the McLaughlin
family dog curse and how--

01:03:59.380 --> 01:04:01.360
Right.
Growing up we had two dogs

01:04:01.360 --> 01:04:04.640
run away and then as an
adult I've had two dogs die

01:04:04.640 --> 01:04:06.580
and then we went for well over a decade

01:04:06.580 --> 01:04:09.750
without having a dog
because of this curse.

01:04:09.750 --> 01:04:14.363
Now Barbara, a few things
about Barbara, number one,

01:04:16.820 --> 01:04:19.990
she's been around significantly
longer for any other dog

01:04:19.990 --> 01:04:22.280
and she's only been around
for like three years or so.

01:04:22.280 --> 01:04:23.600
Mm-hmm.

01:04:23.600 --> 01:04:28.600
Our relationship with her
is way different and better

01:04:28.850 --> 01:04:31.400
than the relationship that
we had with the other dogs.

01:04:31.400 --> 01:04:34.060
I mean Merle, our first dog as a couple

01:04:35.600 --> 01:04:38.733
died in the first year and then Gypsy,

01:04:40.860 --> 01:04:42.690
our second dog who...

01:04:44.820 --> 01:04:46.060
No, maybe it was the other way around.

01:04:46.060 --> 01:04:47.373
Gypsy was the first dog.

01:04:48.700 --> 01:04:51.589
She was a rescue that I
literally found on the side

01:04:51.589 --> 01:04:54.820
of the road and I rescued and kept.

01:04:54.820 --> 01:04:56.720
'Cause she didn't have her owner's number.

01:04:56.720 --> 01:04:58.320
I couldn't call her old owner's number

01:04:58.320 --> 01:04:59.980
so I just kept her for myself.

01:04:59.980 --> 01:05:02.074
No but she was like an
abandoned puppy in the woods

01:05:02.074 --> 01:05:05.400
and she made it like a year and then died

01:05:05.400 --> 01:05:09.860
and when she died, Jessie
was pregnant at the time

01:05:11.000 --> 01:05:13.360
and when she died that
night, she died at the beach.

01:05:13.360 --> 01:05:14.860
She got ran over at the beach.

01:05:16.690 --> 01:05:20.190
And we just sat and laid
in the bed and cried.

01:05:20.190 --> 01:05:22.440
And then the rest of
the trip was kinda like

01:05:23.410 --> 01:05:25.900
clouded by this.
Mm-hmm.

01:05:25.900 --> 01:05:30.900
And then there would be times
over the next couple months

01:05:32.400 --> 01:05:34.470
where we would think about her.

01:05:34.470 --> 01:05:37.101
I would think about
her and I would get sad

01:05:37.101 --> 01:05:42.101
maybe almost cry or get teary-eyed.

01:05:42.836 --> 01:05:46.890
And how was the quality of
relationship that you had

01:05:46.890 --> 01:05:50.000
with Gypsy compared to Barbara now?

01:05:50.000 --> 01:05:52.230
Not even close.
Right.

01:05:52.230 --> 01:05:53.063
So--

01:05:53.980 --> 01:05:57.430
'Cause I think you
approach dogs differently.

01:05:57.430 --> 01:05:59.990
There's all kinds of things
that are different like

01:05:59.990 --> 01:06:01.520
it's been a longer period of time.

01:06:01.520 --> 01:06:05.343
Barbara is, she's a special dog.

01:06:06.870 --> 01:06:11.870
She's the most affectionate
dog I've ever met

01:06:11.870 --> 01:06:15.232
and we have our stretch and
snuggle time in the morning

01:06:15.232 --> 01:06:16.930
when I get on my back and do my stretches

01:06:16.930 --> 01:06:19.770
and she snuggles with
me and then of course,

01:06:19.770 --> 01:06:21.420
our kids--
I didn't observe,

01:06:21.420 --> 01:06:23.610
I don't recall observing
a connection like you have

01:06:23.610 --> 01:06:26.030
with Barbara that--

01:06:26.030 --> 01:06:27.760
It would be devastating.

01:06:27.760 --> 01:06:31.900
I think there's a trope-ish
stage of life thing that--

01:06:31.900 --> 01:06:34.030
For sure.
That dads go through when--

01:06:34.030 --> 01:06:36.722
For sure, I never had a daughter.

01:06:36.722 --> 01:06:39.890
My only daughter is Barbara.
Okay.

01:06:39.890 --> 01:06:42.170
But and I think--
And that was difficult

01:06:42.170 --> 01:06:44.500
but you know how much more
difficult it's gonna be

01:06:44.500 --> 01:06:47.113
if Barbara passes.
It would be devastating

01:06:47.113 --> 01:06:52.113
but and listen, there is
no comparison in my mind,

01:06:54.900 --> 01:06:56.653
there's absolutely no comparison.

01:06:57.600 --> 01:07:00.329
Obviously losing a child would be,

01:07:00.329 --> 01:07:03.230
that's the worst thing that could happen.

01:07:03.230 --> 01:07:04.910
I can't imagine anything worse than that.

01:07:04.910 --> 01:07:07.160
That is the worst thing
that can happen to you.

01:07:07.160 --> 01:07:09.220
And they're not even,
I don't think they're

01:07:09.220 --> 01:07:11.330
in the same ballpark by any means

01:07:11.330 --> 01:07:15.453
because with a dog, you
kinda know this dog is,

01:07:16.350 --> 01:07:21.070
like you said, Jade's existence
and Barbara's existence

01:07:21.070 --> 01:07:24.450
in your family has reached
a stasis that it will

01:07:24.450 --> 01:07:28.800
basically be at until she
starts to get older and feebler.

01:07:28.800 --> 01:07:30.320
But she's pretty much who she's gonna be.

01:07:30.320 --> 01:07:35.320
She's fully expressed and
she's gonna live a maximum

01:07:35.470 --> 01:07:38.955
of something teen years.
Right.

01:07:38.955 --> 01:07:41.430
And you know that that's gonna happen.

01:07:41.430 --> 01:07:43.430
So if it happens earlier,
it would be devastating

01:07:43.430 --> 01:07:46.840
but it's still not a drastic upheaval

01:07:46.840 --> 01:07:49.810
in what you're planning for your dog

01:07:50.720 --> 01:07:53.970
in the way that you plan for
another human in your life.

01:07:53.970 --> 01:07:57.050
And so I think that there's
a period of mourning

01:07:57.050 --> 01:08:01.040
but then you're kinda like
okay, I'm gonna get another dog.

01:08:01.040 --> 01:08:03.390
I don't wanna downplay the mourning

01:08:03.390 --> 01:08:05.930
but I do think that...

01:08:07.632 --> 01:08:11.350
I'm interested in feedback so #EarBiscuits

01:08:11.350 --> 01:08:12.410
but I think...

01:08:15.588 --> 01:08:17.290
And I may have mentioned
this, there are times

01:08:17.290 --> 01:08:19.420
that I think about getting a second dog

01:08:19.420 --> 01:08:23.260
and I do think that that's part of,

01:08:23.260 --> 01:08:26.520
I'm interested in that
being part of the strategy,

01:08:26.520 --> 01:08:31.460
if at a certain point in
introducing a second dog

01:08:31.460 --> 01:08:34.033
so there's like overlap,
there can just be overlap.

01:08:35.420 --> 01:08:39.150
So it's like if Jade's nine,

01:08:42.010 --> 01:08:44.090
not too old.

01:08:44.090 --> 01:08:47.310
Not like okay, I got
like a year or something,

01:08:47.310 --> 01:08:50.230
I gotta try to, more of
an overlap than that.

01:08:50.230 --> 01:08:53.080
I kinda feel like that
would help a little.

01:08:53.080 --> 01:08:56.330
But I could be totally wrong,
I've never been through this.

01:08:56.330 --> 01:08:57.930
I think it does help
'cause we've got friends

01:08:57.930 --> 01:09:02.930
who had dogs that, weren't Max
and Ruby brother and sister?

01:09:03.290 --> 01:09:04.123
Yeah.

01:09:05.143 --> 01:09:09.583
And our friend Nick had two
dogs he was very attached to.

01:09:10.800 --> 01:09:12.213
And he lost one.
He ended up,

01:09:12.213 --> 01:09:13.507
they lost both of them eventually.

01:09:13.507 --> 01:09:16.610
I mean losing the second
one, I don't know,

01:09:16.610 --> 01:09:19.562
I'd have to ask him what was worse but--

01:09:19.562 --> 01:09:22.713
I definitely think that
being able to be like,

01:09:23.760 --> 01:09:25.140
we still got a dog.

01:09:25.140 --> 01:09:29.548
To go from having a dog to
having no dogs I think would be,

01:09:29.548 --> 01:09:33.120
or a pet, obviously this
also applies to cats,

01:09:33.120 --> 01:09:35.030
those of you who can make those kinds

01:09:35.030 --> 01:09:36.793
of emotional connections to cats.

01:09:38.170 --> 01:09:41.130
The same thing applies, in fact, my son--

01:09:41.130 --> 01:09:43.400
Very magnanimous of you--
My son has a friend

01:09:43.400 --> 01:09:47.910
whose cat recently died and it was,

01:09:47.910 --> 01:09:49.540
cat had been around for a very long time

01:09:49.540 --> 01:09:52.360
and the family had a very difficult time.

01:09:52.360 --> 01:09:56.520
Lincoln had a friend who
came to school talking about

01:09:56.520 --> 01:09:59.100
how his dog died and everyone was sad

01:09:59.100 --> 01:10:03.550
and Lincoln cried for his friend and then,

01:10:03.550 --> 01:10:05.950
turns out the dude was joking.

01:10:05.950 --> 01:10:09.590
And then a few weeks later,
his dog actually died.

01:10:09.590 --> 01:10:11.470
Well yeah, that's what happens to you.

01:10:11.470 --> 01:10:13.080
Joke about your dog
dying, it's gonna happen.

01:10:13.080 --> 01:10:13.913
Just like you said--
'Cause of The Secret.

01:10:13.913 --> 01:10:15.930
Manifest.
Yeah you manifest.

01:10:15.930 --> 01:10:18.700
Full circle, that was still this podcast.

01:10:18.700 --> 01:10:20.740
We've been on quite a journey, Rhett.

01:10:20.740 --> 01:10:22.010
Wow he cried for a friend,

01:10:22.010 --> 01:10:24.384
that is a high level of empathy.

01:10:24.384 --> 01:10:26.576
Yeah.

01:10:26.576 --> 01:10:28.560
Hold on in what context?
I'm crying right now.

01:10:28.560 --> 01:10:32.149
He talked about the dog
dying and he started crying

01:10:32.149 --> 01:10:34.285
in front of him or later, he was--

01:10:34.285 --> 01:10:35.670
He teared up.

01:10:35.670 --> 01:10:36.793
That's unusual.

01:10:37.900 --> 01:10:40.500
Yeah, well, it's not unusual for Lincoln,

01:10:40.500 --> 01:10:42.940
I think that's just, you
know, given his connection

01:10:42.940 --> 01:10:44.063
with Jade and--
I'm not saying it's unusual,

01:10:44.063 --> 01:10:47.270
I'm saying it's unusual for
a teenage boy to be told

01:10:47.270 --> 01:10:49.430
about someone else's
dog dying and then cry.

01:10:49.430 --> 01:10:52.380
I'm saying as a father of a teenage boy

01:10:52.380 --> 01:10:55.210
who knows a lot of teenage
boys, I'd say that's unusual.

01:10:55.210 --> 01:10:57.750
I'm saying it's a good
thing but it's not normal.

01:10:57.750 --> 01:11:01.070
It's good, it's like you
would want other kids

01:11:01.070 --> 01:11:02.133
to have that level of empathy.

01:11:02.133 --> 01:11:05.400
Yeah I'm glad he did it
especially just so his friend

01:11:05.400 --> 01:11:07.140
could turn around and stamp on it

01:11:07.140 --> 01:11:08.880
and say he was just joking.

01:11:08.880 --> 01:11:11.870
I'll never cry for your dog again, jerk.

01:11:11.870 --> 01:11:15.250
I did ask him, so when his
dog really did die, did you--

01:11:15.250 --> 01:11:17.380
He laughed, naturally.

01:11:17.380 --> 01:11:19.863
Yeah it's like assuming
that he's joking, it's like,

01:11:21.301 --> 01:11:24.190
and I can have a dark sense of
humor so I reserve the right

01:11:24.190 --> 01:11:25.963
to joke about Jade dying.

01:11:27.290 --> 01:11:28.540
That doesn't, you know--

01:11:28.540 --> 01:11:30.870
And I think that's okay.

01:11:30.870 --> 01:11:33.400
I think it's okay and I
think it demonstrates--

01:11:33.400 --> 01:11:34.780
I think maybe it's part
of my coping mechanism.

01:11:34.780 --> 01:11:36.630
It might be a coping mechanism
but it also demonstrates

01:11:36.630 --> 01:11:39.470
that there's a fundamental
difference between a relationship

01:11:39.470 --> 01:11:41.673
with an animal and a
relationship with a human.

01:11:43.678 --> 01:11:46.705
I could joke about anybody
dying and then feel the,

01:11:46.705 --> 01:11:51.705
I'm not gonna do it currently
but I joke about death.

01:11:52.320 --> 01:11:53.697
And I think it puts it in its place.

01:11:53.697 --> 01:11:56.560
But there are lots of
people who talk about their,

01:11:56.560 --> 01:11:58.780
people who don't have
children who have pets--

01:11:58.780 --> 01:12:01.480
I guess I'm taking up for
Lincoln's friend, I don't know.

01:12:01.480 --> 01:12:04.128
No there are people who don't
have children but have pets

01:12:04.128 --> 01:12:07.350
and sometimes we'll
start talking to people

01:12:07.350 --> 01:12:10.150
who have children and
talking about their pets

01:12:10.150 --> 01:12:12.560
as if they're children,
as if they can relate

01:12:12.560 --> 01:12:14.710
to having children
because they have a pet.

01:12:15.730 --> 01:12:17.860
Pets and children ain't
nearly the same thing.

01:12:17.860 --> 01:12:19.890
But you can get an inkling of that.

01:12:19.890 --> 01:12:22.350
I mean I'm not gonna say
that there's not some--

01:12:22.350 --> 01:12:23.593
No you can't.
Yes you can.

01:12:24.880 --> 01:12:26.640
I highly disagree.

01:12:26.640 --> 01:12:29.812
Having a child and having a pet are not

01:12:29.812 --> 01:12:32.540
in the same ballpark.

01:12:32.540 --> 01:12:34.280
Okay you're responsible for another being.

01:12:34.280 --> 01:12:36.670
Yes that's true, but--

01:12:36.670 --> 01:12:39.220
I'm not minimizing what
happens if you've got a pet.

01:12:39.220 --> 01:12:42.380
I'm saying what you're
saying is true except that

01:12:42.380 --> 01:12:44.705
that there is a connection.

01:12:44.705 --> 01:12:49.705
I think you can lose
something precious to you

01:12:50.010 --> 01:12:53.610
that's not even living and it in some,

01:12:53.610 --> 01:12:57.760
I wouldn't then access
that conversationally

01:12:57.760 --> 01:12:59.260
with someone who's lost a child.

01:12:59.260 --> 01:13:01.010
I'm not saying that, I'm not minimizing,

01:13:01.010 --> 01:13:02.580
what I'm saying is that for those people,

01:13:02.580 --> 01:13:04.700
this is unrelated to the thing dying.

01:13:04.700 --> 01:13:07.090
What I'm saying is is if
you're in a conversation

01:13:07.090 --> 01:13:08.900
with somebody who's got, you're like man,

01:13:08.900 --> 01:13:12.810
I had to, it was kinda crazy,

01:13:12.810 --> 01:13:17.000
my son just went through a
really difficult time at school

01:13:17.000 --> 01:13:18.658
with blah blah blah and they're like yeah,

01:13:18.658 --> 01:13:20.840
my dog has hip dysplasia.

01:13:20.840 --> 01:13:23.621
It's like they're not in the same, okay,

01:13:23.621 --> 01:13:24.930
I'm not saying that's easy,

01:13:24.930 --> 01:13:27.720
but it's not like having a
child go through something.

01:13:27.720 --> 01:13:28.870
It's different, man.

01:13:28.870 --> 01:13:32.423
As someone who's got children
and pets, it's different.

01:13:34.020 --> 01:13:37.920
All I'm saying is I think
people who don't have kids

01:13:37.920 --> 01:13:42.810
can then, they can access
empathy through an experience

01:13:42.810 --> 01:13:44.370
that they have with loving a pet.

01:13:44.370 --> 01:13:45.950
Yeah I don't have a daughter like I said.

01:13:45.950 --> 01:13:47.050
I'm the one who said a second ago

01:13:47.050 --> 01:13:48.200
that Barbara's my daughter.
That's all I'm saying.

01:13:48.200 --> 01:13:50.100
I tell her she could be
anything she wants to be.

01:13:50.100 --> 01:13:51.380
She's gonna go to doggy college,

01:13:51.380 --> 01:13:52.790
she's gonna have a great doggy career.

01:13:52.790 --> 01:13:55.876
Are you going to, what do
you think about the overlap

01:13:55.876 --> 01:13:59.280
in order to prepare, you know--

01:13:59.280 --> 01:14:00.820
It's not worth it.

01:14:00.820 --> 01:14:01.969
Hmm.

01:14:01.969 --> 01:14:06.570
I don't want to deal with two dogs.

01:14:06.570 --> 01:14:07.433
Plain and simple.

01:14:08.450 --> 01:14:09.730
Barbara is--

01:14:09.730 --> 01:14:11.010
I don't wanna divide the love.

01:14:11.010 --> 01:14:13.140
She's attached to humans.

01:14:13.140 --> 01:14:15.420
Well that's not a problem,
you got three kids,

01:14:15.420 --> 01:14:17.610
you didn't divide the love between them.

01:14:17.610 --> 01:14:18.443
Yeah I did.

01:14:19.665 --> 01:14:20.498
No I just don't--
I don't know the

01:14:20.498 --> 01:14:21.930
answer to that.

01:14:21.930 --> 01:14:25.200
When we travel and
figuring out what to do,

01:14:25.200 --> 01:14:28.010
if we're gonna take 'em on
a walk, adding an extra dog

01:14:28.010 --> 01:14:29.787
into the mix is same reason
I've only got two kids

01:14:29.787 --> 01:14:34.040
and not three, so I wanna minimize that.

01:14:34.040 --> 01:14:39.040
I want to enjoy the
benefits of having a pet.

01:14:39.530 --> 01:14:41.270
And then start over afterward.

01:14:41.270 --> 01:14:42.410
And then yeah.

01:14:42.410 --> 01:14:46.540
And maybe take a relatively
long break before the next one.

01:14:46.540 --> 01:14:47.570
I don't know.

01:14:47.570 --> 01:14:48.940
Well--
Depends on what stage

01:14:48.940 --> 01:14:49.840
of life I'm in.

01:14:49.840 --> 01:14:54.277
I think that makes me
think that maybe I should,

01:14:54.277 --> 01:14:58.060
I shouldn't get another dog
in order to not go through

01:14:58.060 --> 01:14:59.620
the pain of losing one.
You should get another dog

01:14:59.620 --> 01:15:01.400
if you want another dog, period.

01:15:01.400 --> 01:15:06.400
Yeah, you shouldn't use
a dog as a death bridge.

01:15:06.560 --> 01:15:08.160
And some people think you
should get another dog,

01:15:08.160 --> 01:15:11.373
depending on the breed, for
the companionship of the dog.

01:15:11.373 --> 01:15:13.030
Like I said--
Our dogs,

01:15:13.030 --> 01:15:15.320
neither one of them
need that, that's clear.

01:15:15.320 --> 01:15:16.153
They have plenty of--

01:15:16.153 --> 01:15:18.563
Our dogs do not know that they're dogs.

01:15:20.120 --> 01:15:21.450
So getting another dog would be like

01:15:21.450 --> 01:15:23.700
what is this dog doing here?

01:15:23.700 --> 01:15:25.360
You know?

01:15:25.360 --> 01:15:26.430
Why did you get a dog?

01:15:26.430 --> 01:15:27.770
Did you get me a dog?

01:15:27.770 --> 01:15:30.072
What made you guys think
that we needed a dog?

01:15:32.180 --> 01:15:33.953
Is what our dogs would say.

01:15:33.953 --> 01:15:37.660
When you said that I
pictured a meme of Barbara

01:15:37.660 --> 01:15:39.100
with another dog.

01:15:39.100 --> 01:15:40.520
Yeah there's a good
meme of that somewhere.

01:15:40.520 --> 01:15:41.353
Yeah.

01:15:42.640 --> 01:15:43.632
It's funny.

01:15:43.632 --> 01:15:45.350
Somebody do that, make a meme.

01:15:45.350 --> 01:15:47.550
But given the time that we've taken--

01:15:47.550 --> 01:15:49.110
Yeah let's wrap it up.
We need to hear your rec.

01:15:49.110 --> 01:15:51.870
Oh my gosh, my rec is...

01:15:54.070 --> 01:15:58.300
I look for an app to
catalog my record collection

01:15:58.300 --> 01:16:01.490
on my phone because now that
I'm back into collection mode,

01:16:01.490 --> 01:16:03.870
I wanna know what that
was and I went around

01:16:03.870 --> 01:16:06.690
to a bunch of cute different
apps and then I came back

01:16:06.690 --> 01:16:10.140
to the one that everyone talks about

01:16:10.140 --> 01:16:14.090
on the vinyl Reddit
thread and that's Discogs.

01:16:14.090 --> 01:16:16.340
Which is kind of a marketplace for buying

01:16:16.340 --> 01:16:21.000
and selling of records
but I just use it to,

01:16:21.000 --> 01:16:24.500
it's a way for me to carry
my record collection with me

01:16:24.500 --> 01:16:26.230
at all times.

01:16:26.230 --> 01:16:28.603
See look, I can just scroll.

01:16:29.650 --> 01:16:31.160
Well I guess the pictures don't show up

01:16:31.160 --> 01:16:34.970
if you're in airport mode but
the pictures usually show up.

01:16:34.970 --> 01:16:37.300
And sometimes when I get
bored and I wanna feel good

01:16:37.300 --> 01:16:38.867
about myself I look at
my record collection

01:16:38.867 --> 01:16:40.550
and I just kinda scroll it.

01:16:40.550 --> 01:16:43.800
Can you click on that--
It boosts my chi.

01:16:43.800 --> 01:16:45.931
Can you click on that and
would this be sacrilegious--

01:16:45.931 --> 01:16:46.764
I don't even know what that means.

01:16:46.764 --> 01:16:47.740
If you clicked on that

01:16:47.740 --> 01:16:50.539
and you could play them on your phone

01:16:50.539 --> 01:16:52.980
like the digital version of those songs

01:16:52.980 --> 01:16:54.503
or does that go against the whole point?

01:16:54.503 --> 01:16:56.303
I think you're describing Spotify.

01:16:57.640 --> 01:16:59.270
No it cannot do that.
No but if you wanted

01:16:59.270 --> 01:17:01.147
to be like, oh this is
my record collection.

01:17:01.147 --> 01:17:03.170
This is the songs that I own.

01:17:03.170 --> 01:17:05.090
Oh I want you to hear this
song but we're not currently

01:17:05.090 --> 01:17:06.330
at my record player.

01:17:06.330 --> 01:17:09.360
That feels like you, yeah,
you could just go one app over

01:17:09.360 --> 01:17:12.640
but if you wanna experience
the album as intended--

01:17:12.640 --> 01:17:14.040
Look at that.

01:17:14.040 --> 01:17:15.720
Yeah you can't do that.

01:17:15.720 --> 01:17:17.237
But you can make a wishlist
and then you can buy--

01:17:17.237 --> 01:17:20.233
How often do you listen
to your record player?

01:17:24.520 --> 01:17:26.020
I'm trying to do it more often

01:17:26.020 --> 01:17:27.150
now that I got these records.

01:17:27.150 --> 01:17:28.790
Because every time I've
come over to your house,

01:17:28.790 --> 01:17:30.880
you're just playing Spotify.

01:17:30.880 --> 01:17:33.770
Yeah 'cause it goes
throughout the whole house.

01:17:33.770 --> 01:17:36.713
It's a different, you have
to have a listening session.

01:17:36.713 --> 01:17:39.060
It takes discipline and
a little bit of planning

01:17:39.060 --> 01:17:43.140
and then, I'll do it
when I'm drinking coffee

01:17:43.140 --> 01:17:44.419
on a weekend morning.

01:17:44.419 --> 01:17:46.300
Mm.
And I've started trying

01:17:46.300 --> 01:17:48.748
to do it like sometimes during
dinner we'll play a record

01:17:48.748 --> 01:17:50.740
but then in the middle
of it I gotta get up

01:17:50.740 --> 01:17:52.838
and flip the record, but all of that--

01:17:52.838 --> 01:17:53.730
You need a jukebox.

01:17:53.730 --> 01:17:56.776
All of that jukebox, all
of that's part of it.

01:17:56.776 --> 01:18:00.980
And it's the negative, the
things that you could point out

01:18:00.980 --> 01:18:04.323
as negative aspects of record listening,

01:18:05.220 --> 01:18:07.860
I'm just trying, I think
you can make 'em positives.

01:18:07.860 --> 01:18:09.923
It's not about the convenience,
it's about the art.

01:18:10.887 --> 01:18:13.400
And if you just wanna get a little boost

01:18:13.400 --> 01:18:16.526
even when you're apart from
your record collection,

01:18:16.526 --> 01:18:19.780
you can get Discogs and if
you're doing some crate diggin',

01:18:19.780 --> 01:18:21.180
you won't buy doubles.

01:18:21.180 --> 01:18:24.280
Do you feel a noticeable,
tangible difference

01:18:24.280 --> 01:18:27.230
between the vinyl songs
and the digital songs?

01:18:27.230 --> 01:18:31.300
Nope and red wine is red
wine but I, you know,

01:18:31.300 --> 01:18:36.010
I know, I aspire to be able
to have an opinion on red wine

01:18:36.010 --> 01:18:40.754
and I kind of, and I
actually did do an AB test.

01:18:40.754 --> 01:18:42.790
Yes, it sounds different.

01:18:42.790 --> 01:18:44.177
If you walked into the room--

01:18:44.177 --> 01:18:46.120
It's warmer.
You would know which one

01:18:46.120 --> 01:18:47.920
was which.
I think I would, yeah.

01:18:49.008 --> 01:18:50.160
Yeah, on the same speakers.
'Cause you'd see

01:18:50.160 --> 01:18:51.573
the record spinning, you would--

01:18:51.573 --> 01:18:53.130
Yeah you can tell a difference.

01:18:53.130 --> 01:18:56.660
You can tell a difference,
but I think being able

01:18:56.660 --> 01:18:59.680
to tell the difference, I'm
not gonna say that makes me

01:18:59.680 --> 01:19:02.777
a cooler or better person but
I am kind of implying that.

01:19:02.777 --> 01:19:05.810
Do you listen to records
and drink red wine

01:19:05.810 --> 01:19:06.760
at the same time?

01:19:06.760 --> 01:19:08.770
I haven't.
Hmm.

01:19:08.770 --> 01:19:09.970
I should.

01:19:09.970 --> 01:19:13.453
But you're gonna be drinking
like Barefoot wines.

01:19:14.320 --> 01:19:16.402
Charles.
Oh.

01:19:16.402 --> 01:19:18.033
I was gonna say Charles Schwab.

01:19:19.930 --> 01:19:22.110
What's two-buck Chuck, Charles what?

01:19:22.110 --> 01:19:25.100
Charles, Chuck, I don't know.

01:19:25.100 --> 01:19:26.400
Charles.
It's the cheapest wine

01:19:26.400 --> 01:19:27.340
you can get.

01:19:27.340 --> 01:19:29.930
I thought when you said
Barefoot I thought you literally

01:19:29.930 --> 01:19:32.200
were talking about because
my feet were so sweaty

01:19:32.200 --> 01:19:33.645
from wearing shoes all day without socks.

01:19:33.645 --> 01:19:35.830
You're barefoot drinking
Barefoot wine listening to--

01:19:35.830 --> 01:19:37.460
Yeah.

01:19:37.460 --> 01:19:39.620
Something barefoot.
Barefoot Mountain Boys.

01:19:39.620 --> 01:19:41.070
Barenaked Ladies, you got--

01:19:42.150 --> 01:19:44.050
Charles Shaw.

01:19:44.050 --> 01:19:46.639
That's my rec, get some Charles Shaw,

01:19:46.639 --> 01:19:49.492
get your records, Discogs.

01:19:49.492 --> 01:19:50.575
#EarBiscuits.

01:19:51.500 --> 01:19:53.840
Thanks for letting us
shoot the breeze with ya.

01:19:53.840 --> 01:19:56.100
Yeah don't give us any
crap about waiting so late

01:19:56.100 --> 01:19:57.673
to answer the question, okay?

01:19:58.960 --> 01:20:00.870
And don't give Rhett any crap about

01:20:00.870 --> 01:20:02.130
the thing about his phone.

01:20:02.130 --> 01:20:03.170
That's right.

01:20:03.170 --> 01:20:06.070
The thing I didn't tell you
is that the replacement phone

01:20:06.070 --> 01:20:09.240
that I got is messed up.

01:20:09.240 --> 01:20:10.500
Not because I put it in water

01:20:10.500 --> 01:20:11.990
'cause I'm not doing that again.

01:20:11.990 --> 01:20:13.120
Because--
Why are you not

01:20:13.120 --> 01:20:13.953
doing that again?

01:20:13.953 --> 01:20:17.490
Well the way insurance
works is they give you

01:20:17.490 --> 01:20:18.730
a refurbished phone.

01:20:18.730 --> 01:20:21.130
Everything about it seems
completely normal and new,

01:20:21.130 --> 01:20:23.680
like you see that screen,
there's not a scratch on it.

01:20:23.680 --> 01:20:26.600
The face ID will not
activate which is a problem

01:20:26.600 --> 01:20:28.440
that is usually a hardware problem.

01:20:28.440 --> 01:20:29.880
So every time you look
at your phone and try

01:20:29.880 --> 01:20:31.690
to get it to activate, you
think about that moment

01:20:31.690 --> 01:20:34.790
when you told me, "I've got this."

01:20:34.790 --> 01:20:36.590
And I'm gonna have to do it again.

01:20:37.510 --> 01:20:39.040
You don't have to say anything.

01:20:39.040 --> 01:20:42.120
This is one of those situations
where I learned my lesson

01:20:42.120 --> 01:20:45.133
without you having to say anything at all.

01:20:46.220 --> 01:20:47.820
Now that you've said that--

01:20:47.820 --> 01:20:49.533
You just couldn't resist.

01:20:51.444 --> 01:20:53.380
Now that you've said
that, I can let it go.

01:20:53.380 --> 01:20:54.530
We'll keep resisting.

